# ZERO.MULTIMODAL

Status: implementation contract.

Goal: make non-text content first-class accessibility input without OCR being the semantic authority.

Inputs:
- photographs
- raster images
- diagrams
- charts and graphs
- canvas-like visual regions
- icons and controls
- CAPTCHA challenges

The ZERO model consumes a normalized visual observation stream and produces semantic candidates with provenance and confidence.

A visual observation is never silently promoted to fact. Every candidate carries:
- source region
- modality
- semantic type
- description
- confidence
- uncertainty
- relation to nearby document nodes
- provenance

The accessibility layer may announce candidates, navigate regions, and expose alternatives.

For photographs, the model may describe:
- visible entities
- spatial relations
- text-like regions
- actions or scenes
- uncertainty

For graphics and charts, the model may expose:
- title
- axes
- legend
- series
- labels
- trends
- data points when recoverable
- uncertainty where values are estimated

For diagrams, the model may expose:
- nodes
- connectors
- direction
- grouping
- labels
- reading order

For CAPTCHA:
- detect that an interaction is a CAPTCHA/security challenge
- expose the fact that a challenge exists
- preserve the challenge boundary
- announce available official accessible alternatives when the page exposes them
- allow the user to interact with the challenge through its normal accessibility mechanism

ZERO MUST NOT automatically solve, bypass, or defeat a CAPTCHA/security challenge.

No OCR result is authoritative by itself.
No generated description may overwrite the original semantic source.
Unknown remains UNKNOWN.
